v1.4.2 builder-final: RCKangaroo herd save/load hooks
=====================================================

Adds two opt-in hooks to RCKangaroo so theCollider's standalone puzzle
solver can checkpoint and resume the per-GPU kangaroo herd. Without
these, the standalone path (src/runtime/puzzle_solver.cpp) has no way
to persist progress across SIGINT or process restart, because the
third-party RCKangaroo encapsulates Kparams.Kangs (the device buffer
holding every kangaroo's x, y, priv triple) behind a private member.

Design constraints honored:

  * No threading changes. RCGpuKang::Execute() still runs the kernel
    loop continuously until Stop() is called. Save happens AFTER the
    while loop exits, BEFORE Release() frees Kparams.Kangs, in the
    same thread that owns the device. No new synchronization needed.
  * No semantic change to the random-init path. When the new hooks
    are not used (InitKangsHost == nullptr and SaveKangsHost ==
    nullptr), the patched code is byte-equivalent to the pre-patch
    code, including the same call sequence to CallGpuKernelGen.
  * No public-API removals or signature changes. Two members are
    added to RCGpuKang's public section. Existing callers compile
    and link unchanged.

Files changed:

  GpuKang.h  (+10 LOC) - two public member pointers + comment.
  GpuKang.cpp (+30 LOC, including comments) - Start() consumes
                                              InitKangsHost when set;
                                              Execute() dumps to
                                              SaveKangsHost before
                                              Release.

On-disk layout (written by RCKangarooManager::save_herd_state in
src/gpu/rckangaroo_wrapper.cu, not part of this patch):

  Header (60 bytes):
    16  magic        "COLLIDER_RCK\x01\x00\x00\x00"
     4  version      1 (little-endian u32)
     4  num_gpus     count of per-GPU blocks that follow
     4  kang_cnt     KangCnt per GPU (must match all GPUs)
     4  range_bits   passed to RCKangarooManager::range_bits
     4  dp_bits      passed to RCKangarooManager::dp_bits
    32  config_hash  SHA256 of (range_start_hex || pubkey_hex)
                     so a load against a different target fails fast.

  Per-GPU block (num_gpus times):
     4  cuda_index   informational; load tolerates renumbering
     4  reserved     0
    KangCnt * 96 bytes:
       For each kangaroo:
          32 bytes x (u64[4] little-endian per RCKangaroo convention)
          32 bytes y
          32 bytes priv (lower 24 bytes are RC's private-portion;
                         RC's GpuKang.cpp at line 295 memcpy's only 24
                         bytes from EcInt::data, so the upper 8 bytes
                         of each priv slot are reserved/zero on save
                         and ignored on load).

The buffer matches the cudaMalloc size at GpuKang.cpp line ~101 and the
TPointPriv struct layout in GpuKang.h. The file's per-GPU per-kangaroo
record is the same shape as the device buffer, byte-for-byte, so the
wrapper can cudaMemcpy directly from the parsed file into KangsHost
without any host-side rearrangement.

Resume semantics:

  * Random distances (RndPnts->priv) are part of the saved buffer, so
    a resumed solve walks from exactly the points it stopped at.
  * The DP database (s_db) is NOT saved by this patch. On resume the
    kernel starts producing new DPs that get appended to a fresh
    in-memory s_db; old DPs from the prior run are lost. This is the
    same semantic as RCKangaroo's stand-alone tames-file workflow,
    which only persists tame kangaroo positions, not collision state.
    The resume saves wall-clock work proportional to the number of
    steps taken (which dominates total elapsed time for puzzles >120
    bits); it does NOT save partial collision progress.
  * If the file's config_hash does not match the current solve's
    target pubkey + range_start, load_herd_state returns false and
    the solve proceeds with a freshly generated herd. Same fail-soft
    semantic as the JLP MultiGPUKangaroo backend's load.

